in his analysis and criticism of racism  in the America in which  african-americans along with with some  allies from other races are struggling  for civil rights particularly in the  south Martin Luther King is going to  treat racism as essentially an arbitrary  and irrational form of inequality and  he's going to spend a lot of time  talking about what the experience of  that is like why is he focusing on that  he wants to dramatize as he's put it he  wants to bring before the public eye  what is actually occurring in the  segregated self in the struggles against  it in the reprisals that are happening  to those who are engaged in in calling  for freedom and justice and equality he  really has his eye on the white  moderates and their calls for you know  law and order and for just allowing time  to take its toll why he's bringing in  all of this is to try to argue  ultimately that the nonviolent action  approach to these matters is the only  one that's truly adequate to dealing  with this he doesn't think that racism  is something that can endure forever in  the form that it's adopted in you know  20th century America and the question is  well what's going to happen instead of  that another way that he's going to put  it is well we're all going to be  extremists so we have to decide what  we're going to be extremists for as well  we'll look at in just a moment let's  talk now about the experiences of racism  that he specifically focuses on in a  letter from a Birmingham jail also to a  certain extent in the I have a dream  speech so he talks about one thing right  off the bat which is physical violence  racism had been not only  you know engaged by physical violence or  embodied by physical violence when  whites are able to attack blacks with  impunity and they have to live in fear  of any sort of reprisal like that  happening they never know when you know  say the the KKK is going to come along  and start burning or pillaging or you  know lynching people but it's also  evident in the fact that that violence  is used against those who protest  against violence so it's sort of like  violence taken to a higher level  violence is there as an ever-present  threat so you know not only is there the  violence of actual lynching going on  still in this in the South but also  police brutality those who are trying to  say hey this is this sort of thing has  to end our encountering a police force  that is you know using pretty extreme  violence against them as well you know  when we talk about turning fire hoses on  people sometimes it's imagine that well  that's just like you know being by a  fire hydrant oh those fire hoses are  high pressure they could knock people  down and break bones and peel skin off  of people's flesh so violence outright  violence is is sort of the naked  expression of racism but there's other  forms of racism that are just as  problematic and just as pervasive that  King is talking about he mentions  economic forms of racism he has this  phrase when you see the vast majority of  your twenty million Negro brothers  smothering in an airtight cage of  poverty in the midst of an affluent  society so you know that's what makes it  particularly galling the fact that you  know in the America that he's writing  about there are jobs available but you  know the jobs will be reserved for white  people a lot of the you know jobs that  are available to African Americans are  the jobs that are not only considered  lower-class but which pay less so it's  more difficult for them to accumulate  you know the capital require  to start a business or to to own their  own home a lot of them were trapped in  economic schemes as we know particularly  in the north in which home ownership was  was something that was dangled in front  of them but also taken away and in the I  have a dream speech King will actually  talk about this as being a problem of  social mobility he says we cannot be  satisfied as long as the Negro's basic  mobility is from a smaller ghetto to a  larger one so you know what's he talking  about there the fact that even if there  is some opportunities opened for  african-americans and there weren't all  that many at that time even if it widens  a bit it's still moving from one you  know smaller ghetto a place of  confinement a place of not being able to  break out of not being able to move up  to a slightly larger one or a slightly  larger one so long as there are still  impediments to as we call it equal  opportunity King says we're going to  have this this problem of racism social  he talks quite a bit about this again  going back to the I have a dream speech  in the line right after that he says we  can never be satisfied as long as our  children are stripped of their adulthood  and robbed of their dignity by science  stating for white only segregation had  an incredible human effect and he talks  about this in a very personal way as  well in a letter from a Birmingham jail  he says when you find your tongue  twisted and your speech stammering as  you explain to your six-year-old  daughter why she can't go to the public  amusement park that has just been  advertised on television and see tears  welling up in her eyes when she's told  that Funtown is closed to colored  children and he goes beyond just the you  know immediate emotional impact of that  itself he talks about a longer more  lasting development of this he says you  see ominous clouds of inferiority  beginning to form in her little mental  sky and you see her beginning to distort  her personality by developing an  unconscious  bitterness towards white people when you  have to concoct an answer for a  five-year-old son who is asking daddy  why do white people treat colored people  so mean when you take a cross-country  drive and find it necessary to sleep  night after night in the corners of your  automobile because no motel will accept  you when you're humiliated day in and  day out by nagging signs reading white  and colored so this this social  segregation imposes an incredible human  toll yeah it'll talk about it in other  places it gives not only an inflated  sense of superiority to the whites who  probably shouldn't have that because  very few of them have the content of  character to pull that off but it gives  a sense of inferiority being constantly  pushed on to African Americans by sign  after sign after sign by going from here  to here to here having the same message  constantly reinforced to them that they  they don't matter  politically this is this is representing  talks about this as one of the forms of  unjust laws by disenfranchisement by  African Americans being excluded from  the voting process through all sorts of  different techniques that were being  used in the South you know from poll  taxes to outright intimidation at voting  booths not allowing people to register  yeah he says that the outcome of all of  this is a it has an effect if it has a  you might say emotional an unconscious  in some cases effect of causing fears  they never know when you know the the  rioters are going to come through and  destroy everything that they've got they  know that the police aren't going to  protect them if that happens it also  produces resentments sort of sense of  you know those people are all bad  they're all trying to a process in part  because they experienced that and he  talks about this pervasive sense of  nobody miss a degenerating sense of  nobody  a real person of being less than human  this is what King says this is the  common experience of those who are  subject to racism this form of  inequality that has no legitimate basis  king by the way is not saying that there  can't be any sort of inequality when  he's talking in the I have a dream  speech about his children being judged  by the content of their character he's  perfectly willing to say that if they  have a bad character call them a bad  person say that you know they can't hold  public office if they're subject to  corruption but it shouldn't be based on  the color of their skin why because that  is completely arbitrary it has nothing  to do with the quality of the person it  has nothing to do with their capacities  it has nothing to do with who they  really are when we look at the the  spectrum of perspectives that are  possible to adopt in this King of course  is arguing for for nonviolent action and  you know we see why he gives you a lot  of argument for that but he also  discusses some of the other  possibilities as well so he I'm going to  come back to the white moderate in just  a moment he talks about those within the  African American community who are  proposing in you know engaging in  violent action and you know everything  ranging from self-defense you know  getting weapons to protect themselves  organizing something like a militia that  would be a response to say the the KKK  all the way to those who are advocating  a kind of domestic terrorism he would  include in their groups like you know  black separatists who are arguing that  blacks and whites could never live in an  integrated society that both have to  look out for themselves  some proposals actually went so far that  yes to say that there should be mass  relocation programs that a portion of  the United States should be set aside  for African Americans and African  Americans would all move there and  everybody else would move out and then  the other portion would be left for  white people and King is saying look  none of that is going to work but that's  where things are headed  if this isn't addressed racism is not  going to go away by itself and if you  don't go along with nonviolent action to  try to address racism and ultimately to  eliminate it then pretty soon this is  going to be the alternative that I get  seen as the effective it won't be just  it won't be loving but it might be  effective for a short time probably not  all that effective but to some degree  and it's something that's attractive and  this is actually a struggle that you see  going on within the civil rights  community King is the representative of  non-violent action other people are the  representatives of violent action of  taking reprisals and what is the source  of this he says that you know this is  this is a force within the African  American community says the other force  is one of bitterness and hatred it's  expressed in these particular groups  nourished by the Negroes frustration  over the continued existence of racial  discrimination this movement has made  made up of people who have lost faith in  America who say look we're never  actually going to get a payment on that  blank check that you're talking about in  me I have a dream speech was actually  coming later the people who are saying  we're never going to get equal treatment  we're never going to get what was  promised to us along with this are the  segregationist whites there are plenty  of whites who are saying hey we like  this system just as it is let's not  change anything let's keep everything in  place and then these two can sort of  square off against each other  we also have other possibilities as well  he talks about the forces of  african-american complacency not only  African Americans were engaging in  violent action or nonviolent action in  fact many of them were saying let's not  rock the boat things are you know okay  or they could actually get much much  much much worse so let's not actually do  anything about it King talks about these  as being made up in current made up in  part of Negros who has a law or as a  result of long years of oppression are  so drained  I put beaten down here so drained of  self respect in the sense of somebody  Ness that they've adjusted to  segregation so some people you know that  sense of nobody this comes to be who  they are they come to accept the  oppression they come to accept the  economic exploitation as just the way  things are going to have to be and they  see no point in trying to struggle  against it there's another group who he  says also are engaged in this and that's  the black middle-class he says that a  few middle-class Negroes because of a  degree of academic and economic security  and because in some ways they profit by  segregation they become insensitive to  the problems of the masses so if you can  be insulated against the effects of  segregation by being part of the power  structure but I say being a token  involved in say a university or you know  being the the one token person in a  police force it becomes very difficult  to identify with those who are oppressed  and to say look they have a legitimate  beef because if you did that then you  would actually have to give up your  you're privileged position and take a  stand for that but where King is really  critical is of the white moderates now  why is he so critical of white moderates  in this this piece that's an interesting  question to ask and the answer to that  is he's not actually going to reach  these guys and he's probably not going  to reach these guys but there is a very  strong chance that  those reading the letter from a  Birmingham jail who fall into the  category of white moderates might  actually hear his argument and say you  know what I've been wrong I have to  change my position on all of this stuff  at this point in time so you know how  does he characterize white moderates he  says that they're too much concerned  with law and order he says that where is  this says I hope the white moderate  would understand that law and order  exists for the purpose of establishing  justice when they fail in this purpose  they become the dams that block the flow  of social progress I hope that the white  moderate would understand that the  present tension in the south is a  necessary phase of the transition from  an obnoxious negative peace in which the  Negro passively accepted as unjust  plight to a substantive and positive  peace in 